23:15:09 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: That’s a great question, and you’re right that there was a loud and influential chorus of skepticism in the 1990s. But it’s important to clarify: not *everyone* thought it was a fad—plenty of early adopters, tech companies, and investors were wildly optimistic. However, the skeptics had some very reasonable arguments based on what the Internet looked like at the time. Here’s why many people thought it would fail massively: ### 1. The Experience Was Awful In the early- to mid‑1990s, the Internet (and the Web) was incredibly slow, clunky, and hard to use. You had dial‑up modems screaming, pages loading line by line, and connections dropping constantly. For most people, it felt like a painful downgrade from existing technologies—why wait minutes to read a single news article when you could just pick up a newspaper? The user experience was so bad that many couldn’t imagine it ever becoming mainstream. ### 2. No Clear Business Model The early Web was dominated by hobbyists, academics, and non‑commercial content. Businesses couldn’t figure out how to make money. Gimmicky experiments (like ordering pizza from a computer or watching a grainy video clip) seemed like parlor tricks, not sustainable commerce. Skeptics asked, “How will anyone pay for this?” The dot‑com bubble later inflated on the *hope* of future profits, but when it burst in 2000, the skeptics felt vindicated—at least temporarily. ### 3. Historical Precedent of Overhyped Tech The 1990s had seen a long list of “revolutions” that fizzled: CB radio in the 70s, interactive TV, early videophones, and even early home computers that were sold as “recipe keepers.” The Internet looked to many like another overpromised technology that would burn brightly among enthusiasts and then flame out. Skeptics pointed to the massive hype around the “information superhighway” and guessed it would meet the same fate. ### 4. Famous, Influential Critics Some very smart people dismissed the Internet in memorable ways that still haunt them today: - **Clifford Stoll**, an astronomer and computer expert, wrote a 1995 *Newsweek* essay titled “Why the Web Won’t Be Nirvana,” arguing that online shopping and e‑books would never take off because people want to touch books and smell the bookstore. He famously declared, “No online database will replace your daily newspaper.” - **Paul Krugman** (1998) said, “By 2005 or so, it will become clear that the Internet’s impact on the economy has been no greater than the fax machine’s.” - **Robert Metcalfe**, inventor of Ethernet, predicted in late 1995 that the Internet would “go spectacularly supernova and in 1996 catastrophically collapse.” (He later ate his words—literally blending a printed copy of his column and drinking it.) These voices carried weight and reinforced the idea that the Internet was a fad inflated by irrational exuberance. ### 5. It Seemed Like a Niche Toy For years, the Internet was mostly used by academics, researchers, and a small tribe of techies. Mainstream media portrayed it as a way to chat with strangers, look up useless trivia, or download a pirated song after an hour of waiting. The idea that everyone would one day carry a supercomputer in their pocket, do their banking online, and stream 4K movies was science fiction. It’s easy to forget how hard it was to imagine the *use cases* we now take for granted. ### 6. Privacy and Security Concerns Many people feared that giving their credit card or personal information to a website was insane—and in the early days, they were often right. The technology wasn’t secure, and stories of hackers and viruses made the Internet seem like a dangerous Wild West that sensible people would avoid. ### So, Were They Stupid? No—they were judging the Internet of 1995 by the standards of the real world at that moment. They underestimated how fast infrastructure (broadband), hardware (better modems, faster chips), software (graphical browsers like Mosaic/Netscape), and business innovation would solve those problems. The skeptics were mostly wrong in the long run, but their concerns about the early Web were not crazy. In fact, many of their warnings—about privacy erosion, misinformation, and the value of physical community—turned out to be prescient in a different way. If you’re curious, I can pull up some of those famous “wrong” predictions—they’re hilarious and humbling to read today.
