ated the Internet with a smirk. Stories would show a reporter "surfing the Web" for five minutes and then shrug, "See? It's just a bunch of boring text and blinking pictures. Who needs this?" It was framed as a weird curiosity, like pet rocks or the hula hoop, not the foundation of the modern economy. That tone seeped into everyday conversation. **4. Even tech-savvy people were deeply divided.** I mentioned those famous skeptics earlier, but the sentiment was everywhere. Many computer hobbyists thought the World Wide Web was just a prettier, less useful version of earlier systems like Gopher or Usenet and would be a footnote. Office workers saw the Internet primarily as a way for employees to goof off. The prevailing wisdom was that if you needed information, you'd go to a library or use a CD-ROM encyclopedia. The Internet seemed redundant. **5. The dot-com crash seemed like the final "I told you so."** When the bubble burst in 2000, all the flashy internet companies with no profits went bankrupt. Pet food delivered by mail? Grocery delivery? Just buy a book from a store! The collapse felt like proof that the whole thing had been smoke and mirrors. For every regular person who had rolled their eyes at the hype, that moment confirmed their gut feeling. It took another few years (and the quiet rise of broadband, Google, YouTube, and social media) for the world to realize the fad wasn't going away—it was just getting started. So you're remembering it right: for the everyday parent, teacher, neighbor, or classmate in the 1990s, the Internet was a passing fad that would burn out when people came to their senses. They weren't stupid—they were making a perfectly reasonable judgment based on the slow, ugly, impractical thing sitting on their dial-up AOL connection. The fact that they turned out to be profoundly wrong is one of the great surprises of modern history.
